{
 "fps": 30,
 "lead": 0.8,
 "tail": 2.0,
 "footer": "Sources: canada.ca \u00b7 CRA T4127 Payroll Deductions Formulas 124th ed. (Jan 1 2027) and 122nd ed. (Jan 2026); CCB how much; Canada Groceries and Essentials Benefit; ESDC EI 2027 rate; CRA CPP rates; MP/RRSP limits \u00b7 Examples, general info, not advice",
 "vo": [
  {
   "id": "L1",
   "file": "vo/L1.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "On January first, twenty twenty-seven, every federal tax bracket in Canada moves up. Your salary doesn't have to change at all."
  },
  {
   "id": "L2",
   "file": "vo/L2.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Earn seventy-five thousand dollars in both years, and your federal tax goes down by about one hundred and fifty-nine dollars."
  },
  {
   "id": "L3",
   "file": "vo/L3.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "But in three provinces, the provincial tax-free basic amount doesn't move at all. And in two of them, the brackets are frozen too."
  },
  {
   "id": "L4",
   "file": "vo/L4.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "So who wins in twenty twenty-seven, who stands still, and what changes for families with kids? Let's go through it, number by number."
  },
  {
   "id": "L5",
   "file": "vo/L5.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "All numbers come from the C R A's payroll formulas for January twenty twenty-seven, and from canada dot c a. Examples, not real people. General information, not advice."
  },
  {
   "id": "L6",
   "file": "vo/L6.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "First: why the brackets move at all."
  },
  {
   "id": "L7",
   "file": "vo/L7.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Every year, the federal tax brackets and most credits are indexed to inflation."
  },
  {
   "id": "L8",
   "file": "vo/L8.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "For twenty twenty-seven, the federal indexation rate is two point six percent."
  },
  {
   "id": "L9",
   "file": "vo/L9.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "That means every bracket line moves up by two point six percent. And so does the basic personal amount."
  },
  {
   "id": "L10",
   "file": "vo/L10.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Here are the new federal brackets for twenty twenty-seven."
  },
  {
   "id": "L11",
   "file": "vo/L11.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Fourteen percent on the first sixty thousand and forty-four dollars."
  },
  {
   "id": "L12",
   "file": "vo/L12.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Twenty point five percent up to one hundred and twenty thousand and eighty-eight."
  },
  {
   "id": "L13",
   "file": "vo/L13.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Twenty-six percent up to one hundred and eighty-six thousand, one hundred and fifty-seven."
  },
  {
   "id": "L14",
   "file": "vo/L14.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Twenty-nine percent up to two hundred and sixty-five thousand, two hundred and three."
  },
  {
   "id": "L15",
   "file": "vo/L15.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "And thirty-three percent above that."
  },
  {
   "id": "L16",
   "file": "vo/L16.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "The rates themselves don't change. Only the lines move."
  },
  {
   "id": "L17",
   "file": "vo/L17.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "And the basic personal amount goes from sixteen thousand, four hundred and fifty-two dollars, to sixteen thousand, eight hundred and seventy-nine."
  },
  {
   "id": "L18",
   "file": "vo/L18.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "That's four hundred and twenty-seven dollars more you can earn before federal tax starts. Worth about sixty dollars."
  },
  {
   "id": "L19",
   "file": "vo/L19.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Now, what does that mean for you?"
  },
  {
   "id": "L20",
   "file": "vo/L20.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Let's keep the salary exactly the same, and only change the year. Federal tax only, before other credits."
  },
  {
   "id": "L21",
   "file": "vo/L21.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "At fifty thousand dollars, you save about sixty dollars."
  },
  {
   "id": "L22",
   "file": "vo/L22.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "At seventy-five thousand, about one hundred and fifty-nine."
  },
  {
   "id": "L23",
   "file": "vo/L23.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "At one hundred thousand, also about one hundred and fifty-nine."
  },
  {
   "id": "L24",
   "file": "vo/L24.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "At one hundred and fifty thousand, about three hundred and twenty-six."
  },
  {
   "id": "L25",
   "file": "vo/L25.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Not life-changing. But here's the real point."
  },
  {
   "id": "L26",
   "file": "vo/L26.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Say your salary goes up by exactly two point six percent. From seventy-five thousand, to seventy-six thousand, nine hundred and fifty."
  },
  {
   "id": "L27",
   "file": "vo/L27.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Your federal tax goes up too. From about nine thousand, two hundred and sixty-eight, to about nine thousand, five hundred and nine."
  },
  {
   "id": "L28",
   "file": "vo/L28.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "But it rises in step with your pay. That's what indexing does. It stops inflation from quietly pushing you into a higher bracket."
  },
  {
   "id": "L29",
   "file": "vo/L29.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "That has a name. Bracket creep. And in twenty twenty-seven, not every province protects you from it."
  },
  {
   "id": "L30",
   "file": "vo/L30.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Here's where it gets interesting."
  },
  {
   "id": "L31",
   "file": "vo/L31.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Provinces set their own brackets. And their own indexing."
  },
  {
   "id": "L32",
   "file": "vo/L32.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Ontario indexes by two point one percent. The first bracket line moves from fifty-three thousand, eight hundred and ninety-one, to fifty-five thousand and twenty-three. The basic amount goes up by two hundred and seventy-two dollars."
  },
  {
   "id": "L33",
   "file": "vo/L33.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Alberta caps its indexing at two percent. Its first line moves from sixty-one thousand, two hundred, to sixty-two thousand, four hundred and twenty-four."
  },
  {
   "id": "L34",
   "file": "vo/L34.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Saskatchewan indexes by two point six percent. And then adds an extra five hundred dollars to its basic amount. It goes from twenty thousand, three hundred and eighty-one, to twenty-one thousand, four hundred and eleven."
  },
  {
   "id": "L35",
   "file": "vo/L35.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Nova Scotia indexes by three point five percent. Its basic amount rises to twelve thousand, three hundred and fifty."
  },
  {
   "id": "L36",
   "file": "vo/L36.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "New Brunswick: two point six percent. Fourteen thousand and nineteen."
  },
  {
   "id": "L37",
   "file": "vo/L37.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Now, the three that stand still."
  },
  {
   "id": "L38",
   "file": "vo/L38.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "British Columbia has paused indexing for its brackets and most credits. From twenty twenty-seven to twenty thirty, they stay at twenty twenty-six levels."
  },
  {
   "id": "L39",
   "file": "vo/L39.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Manitoba isn't indexing its brackets or its basic amount either. The lines stay at forty-seven thousand and one hundred thousand."
  },
  {
   "id": "L40",
   "file": "vo/L40.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "And Prince Edward Island's basic amount stays at fifteen thousand dollars."
  },
  {
   "id": "L41",
   "file": "vo/L41.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "So what is the bigger basic amount worth, province by province?"
  },
  {
   "id": "L42",
   "file": "vo/L42.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "In Saskatchewan, about one hundred and eight dollars."
  },
  {
   "id": "L43",
   "file": "vo/L43.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "In Nova Scotia, about thirty-seven. In Alberta, about thirty-six. In New Brunswick, about thirty-three. In Ontario, about fourteen."
  },
  {
   "id": "L44",
   "file": "vo/L44.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "In British Columbia, Manitoba, and Prince Edward Island: zero."
  },
  {
   "id": "L45",
   "file": "vo/L45.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "And in B C and Manitoba, where the brackets are frozen, a raise can push more of your income into a higher provincial bracket. That's bracket creep at work."
  },
  {
   "id": "L46",
   "file": "vo/L46.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Now, families. Because some of the biggest numbers aren't in the brackets at all."
  },
  {
   "id": "L47",
   "file": "vo/L47.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "The Canada child benefit is recalculated every July. And it's indexed to inflation."
  },
  {
   "id": "L48",
   "file": "vo/L48.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Right now, from July twenty twenty-six to June twenty twenty-seven, the maximum is eight thousand, one hundred and fifty-seven dollars a year for each child under six."
  },
  {
   "id": "L49",
   "file": "vo/L49.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "And six thousand, eight hundred and eighty-three dollars for each child from six to seventeen."
  },
  {
   "id": "L50",
   "file": "vo/L50.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "The full amount goes to families with adjusted family net income under thirty-eight thousand, two hundred and thirty-seven dollars. Above that, it starts to shrink."
  },
  {
   "id": "L51",
   "file": "vo/L51.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "These payments are based on your twenty twenty-five return."
  },
  {
   "id": "L52",
   "file": "vo/L52.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "And here's the key for twenty twenty-seven: the payments that start in July twenty twenty-seven will be based on your twenty twenty-six return. So file it on time."
  },
  {
   "id": "L53",
   "file": "vo/L53.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "If your child is eligible for the disability tax credit, the child disability benefit can add up to three thousand, four hundred and eighty dollars a year."
  },
  {
   "id": "L54",
   "file": "vo/L54.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "And the G S T credit has a new name. Since July twenty twenty-six, it's the Canada Groceries and Essentials Benefit."
  },
  {
   "id": "L55",
   "file": "vo/L55.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Up to six hundred and seventy-nine dollars for a single person. Up to eight hundred and ninety for a couple. Plus up to two hundred and thirty-four for each child under nineteen."
  },
  {
   "id": "L56",
   "file": "vo/L56.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "That includes a temporary twenty-five percent increase, set for five years."
  },
  {
   "id": "L57",
   "file": "vo/L57.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Now, your paycheque in January twenty twenty-seven."
  },
  {
   "id": "L58",
   "file": "vo/L58.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "C P P: the first earnings ceiling rises to seventy-six thousand, seven hundred dollars. The second one, to eighty-seven thousand, four hundred."
  },
  {
   "id": "L59",
   "file": "vo/L59.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "E I: the rate is one dollar and sixty-four cents per one hundred dollars. On up to seventy thousand, eight hundred dollars. A maximum of one thousand, one hundred and sixty-one dollars and twelve cents."
  },
  {
   "id": "L60",
   "file": "vo/L60.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "So if you earn above those ceilings, your January paycheque can be a little smaller than your December one."
  },
  {
   "id": "L61",
   "file": "vo/L61.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "And for savers: the R R S P dollar limit for twenty twenty-seven is thirty-five thousand, three hundred and ninety dollars."
  },
  {
   "id": "L62",
   "file": "vo/L62.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "The T F S A limit for twenty twenty-seven wasn't on canada dot c a yet when we checked. Watch for it."
  },
  {
   "id": "L63",
   "file": "vo/L63.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "So here's your twenty twenty-seven checklist."
  },
  {
   "id": "L64",
   "file": "vo/L64.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "One. Check your first paycheque in January. C P P and E I start over, with new ceilings."
  },
  {
   "id": "L65",
   "file": "vo/L65.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Two. If you live in B C, Manitoba, or P E I, expect less inflation relief on the provincial side."
  },
  {
   "id": "L66",
   "file": "vo/L66.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Three. Families: file your twenty twenty-six return on time. It sets your child benefit from July twenty twenty-seven."
  },
  {
   "id": "L67",
   "file": "vo/L67.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Four. Check the new Canada Groceries and Essentials Benefit. It's based on your tax return. So again: file."
  },
  {
   "id": "L68",
   "file": "vo/L68.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Five. Update your R R S P plan with the new limit, and your T F S A when it's announced."
  },
  {
   "id": "L69",
   "file": "vo/L69.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "So, what did we learn?"
  },
  {
   "id": "L70",
   "file": "vo/L70.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "One. In twenty twenty-seven, federal brackets and the basic personal amount rise by two point six percent. The rates stay the same."
  },
  {
   "id": "L71",
   "file": "vo/L71.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Two. Same salary means a little less federal tax. A raise that keeps up with inflation means your tax just keeps up too."
  },
  {
   "id": "L72",
   "file": "vo/L72.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Three. Provinces don't move together. Saskatchewan adds an extra five hundred dollars. B C, Manitoba, and P E I keep their basic amounts frozen."
  },
  {
   "id": "L73",
   "file": "vo/L73.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Four. For families, the biggest numbers are the child benefit and the new groceries benefit. And both start with filing your return."
  },
  {
   "id": "L74",
   "file": "vo/L74.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Next on this channel: the benefits your aging parents could be missing. Subscribe, so you don't miss it."
  }
 ],
